    <h2>Verse Selection Rationale</h2>
    <p>Today is Ravivāra — the Sun's own day — and the verse selected dethrones the sun as light's source: na tad bhāsayate sūryaḥ, the abode the sun cannot illumine. The day's devatā points beyond itself; the sky arranges the lesson. Punarvasu, "the return of light," holds until 2:43 PM — and it is Aditi's star, the mother of the Ādityas, the sun-gods themselves. The mother of all suns carries the teaching: the light that returns each cycle is borrowed; the abode needs none. Yesterday's verse (BG 15.3–4) cut the aśvattha tree and commanded the search for "that place from which having gone, none return" — today 15.6 describes exactly that place, and the two-day arc completes: severance, then revelation. At 2:43 PM, Puṣya joins Sunday to form Ravi Pushya and Sarvartha Siddhi — "the accomplishment of all purposes" — which is the abode's other name, for one who reaches it wants nothing further: wanting itself was the symptom of distance. Śiva yoga (auspicious) replaces yesterday's Parigha (the iron bar) — the residue of the Kalashtami cut is now peace. The Moon enters Karka at 9:01 AM — Cancer, its own sign, the sign of shelter and home — while the tithi observance is Avidhava Navami, and the waning Moon itself performs the teaching: all borrowed light diminishes toward Amāvāsyā. The dhāma does not.</p>
